    "https://vacancies.maastrichtuniversity.nl/job/Maastricht-3D-Digital-Heritage-Researcher-and-Technologist/1373657557/": {
      "title": "3D Digital Heritage Researcher and Technologist",
      "description": "3D Digital Heritage Researcher and Technologist Job Details | Maastricht University Skip to main content Language English Nederlands View Profile Language English Nederlands View Profile All jobs Working at Maastricht University Language English Nederlands View Profile Search by Keyword Show More Options Loading... Faculty/Service Center All Job Type All Location All FTE min All FTE max All Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » 3D Digital Heritage Researcher and Technologist Welcome to Maastricht University! PURE3D 2.0 is creating the world’s first open, sustainable, and FAIR-compliant infrastructure for 3D Scholarly Editions. We are looking for an experienced 3D Heritage Researcher with 3D modelling skills and coding knowledge to act as a bridge between humanities and social science researchers, cultural heritage professionals, and software developers. You will assist with making and refining 3D scholarly models as well as help ensure that the needs of research communities are translated into robust technical specifications and that new infrastructure developments remain grounded in authentic scholarly workflows and requirements. 3D Digital Heritage Researcher and Technologist Our goal: PURE3D 2.0 is an Open Science NL Infrastructure project (2026–2029). We are building a modular, interoperable, open-source platform (by extending the first phase of PURE3D ) for creating and publishing 3D research outputs that are richly annotated, FAIR-compliant, and peer-reviewed, serving academics, GLAM professionals, educators, and the public across Europe. Your colleagues: You will join a dynamic, international team led by Dr Costas Papadopoulos (Maastricht University) and including partners from the University of Amsterdam, TU Delft, Open University, the Netherlands eScience Center, and DANS, as well as collaboration partners such as the Smithsonian Institution, DARIAH-EU, Europeana, the Rijksmuseum, and the National Library of the Netherlands. Within UM’s Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences (FASoS), you will work closely with two Senior RSEs (based at NL eScience Center), a Digital Content and Community Building Specialist, a research assistant, and the project’s PI and Project Manager. What you do Co-design with user communities: Actively develop and participate in co-creation labs, hackathons, training and testing sessions with scholars, GLAM professionals, and educators, developing and translating user requirements into technical specifications and feeding findings back into the development cycle. Conduct applied research on technologies and platforms: Investigate, evaluate, and benchmark emerging 3D web technologies, annotation frameworks, simulation engines, and repository solutions, and produce evidence-based recommendations that steer architectural decisions. Feature Prototyping: Rapid prototyping of possible 3D viewer enhancements for users to give initial feedback before it enters the development process. 3D technical support: Assist project partners on 3D heritage workflows, including digitisation, geometric and material model optimisation and troubleshooting. 3D web standards and practices: Stay current with the rapidly evolving landscape of tools and frameworks such as 3DHOP, ATON, Kompakkt, HBIM, and cloud-based 3D pipelines as well as the use of LLMs both in the coding ecosystem and in 3D workflows. Contribute to the open-source ecosystem: Collaborate with the Smithsonian Institution on extending the Voyager toolkit, while collaborating with PURE3D developers to enhance the PURE3D infrastructure, including assisting with the design of plug-and-play components for 3D model ingestion, quality assurance, versioning, and archiving. Are you ready to build an infrastructure for the future of 3D Scholarship? Then we’d love to meet you. What you bring We’re not looking for a 3D Digital Heritage Researcher and Technologist that is checking all the boxes. We are interested in who you are and what you bring. You are a collaborative and self-directed professional who enjoys working at the intersection of research, cultural heritage, and technology. You can move confidently between scholarly and technical environments, build productive relationships with different communities, and explain complex technical choices clearly to non-technical audiences. You combine strategic thinking with attention to detail and are comfortable managing several activities in a dynamic, international, and cross-institutional project. To succeed in this role, you bring: An Advanced degree and relevant professional experience: An MA/MSc or PhD in the digital humanities, humanities, social sciences, digital heritage, or a related field, or equivalent professional experience, together with at least three years of relevant experience in digital heritage, digital scholarship, research infrastructures, or a closely related area. Practical experience with 3D heritage workflows: Demonstrable experience with one or more aspects of 3D heritage production, analysis, or interpretation, such as photogrammetry, Structure-from-Motion, laser scanning, 3D modelling, Heritage Building Information Modelling (HBIM), digital reconstruction, model optimisation, visualisation, simulation, or scholarly interpretation. Knowledge of 3D web technologies and prototyping: Demonstrable technical knowledge to evaluate and experiment with 3D web technologies, create mock-ups for (elements of) 3D viewers and tools. The candidate should be able to discuss technical options and contribute to technical design discussions. Relevant technologies or platforms a candidate may have experience with include JavaScript, Three.js, WebGL, WebXR, Smithsonian Voyager, 3DHOP, ATON, Kompakkt, or comparable solutions. Experience with iterative and user-centred development: Demonstrable experience gathering and analysing user requirements, involving different communities in design or testing processes, and translating research or user needs into specifications that can be discussed and implemented with software developers. Experience working across disciplines and stakeholder groups: The ability to collaborate effectively with researchers, research software engineers, cultural heritage professionals, educators, students, and institutional partners in multi-partner projects or comparable environments. Excellent communication and organisational skills: Excellent written and spoken English (minimum C1 level - CEFR), with the ability to communicate technical and scholarly matters to different audiences, manage concurrent activities, and maintain high standards of documentation and quality. Knowledge of Dutch is advantageous but not required. We do not expect you to have experience with every technology, platform, or interoperability standard mentioned in this vacancy. Familiarity with open science, FAIR data, repositories, APIs, persistent identifiers, linked data, web annotation standards, paradata, HBIM, or open-source development will be valuable, but you will also have opportunities to develop this knowledge as part of the role. Please note that living in or relocating to Maastricht or its surrounding area is an essential requirement for this position, as regular on-site presence is required. What we offer At Maastricht University, you’ll work in an international, open, and engaged environment. We offer: A 12-month contract with the prospect of an extension until the end of the project (June 2029), based on mutual satisfaction. A gross monthly salary (Scale 11 or 12 depending on experience) between € 4.925 and € 5.932 (please note: the salary range in this description is the correct one and should be used as a reference) based on full-time employment of 38 hours per week. 8% holiday allowance and an 8.3% year-end bonus. 29 vacation days (based on full-time), four additional days off (Carnival Monday and Tuesday, Good Friday, and Liberation Day), and the possibility to accrue up to 12 extra days through compensation hours. Flexible working hours, a home office allowance, and the option to work (partially) from home. Freedom and space to shape your work independently and develop your ideas. A close-knit community of colleagues to collaborate and grow with. A solid pension plan via ABP, company fitness schemes, and access to various university sports facilities. An inspiring work environment in the heart of Europe. About the Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences (FASoS) FASoS is a close-knit, international community of thinkers, doers, and connectors. We offer interdisciplinary education in cultural and social sciences, and our research focuses on societies in transition. Creativity and collaboration are central; ranging from European integration, digitalization, and culture to innovative educational projects. FASoS is small-scale, informal, and diverse, with room to follow your own path. Interested? Want to know more about this position or what it's like to work at our university? Reach out to Dr Costas Papadopoulos at k.papadopoulos@maastrichtuniversity.nl. The deadline for submitting your application is 22 October 2026. Please note the following as part of the selection process: Please upload your CV and a motivation letter (max. 2 pages) explaining why you are interested in this specific role and project, and what you could bring to the project and team. Please also provide a portfolio or code repository link showcasing relevant software projects and briefly explain your contribution to each project. If you have published research in this field, please also upload one of your publications. Interviews will be held in two rounds. The first round for the shortlisted candidates will be on 29 and 30 October. A small number of candidates will be invites for the second round on 6 November, which will also determine the person that will be appointed. A presentation and a technical assessment exercise may be part of the selection procedure. Apply now via the button below. We look forward to getting to know you! About Maastricht University At Maastricht University, we collaboratively seek solutions to help move the world forward. We do this with 23,300 students and 5,400 employees across 5 regional locations, 6 faculties, and more than 70 research institutes. We encourage you to push boundaries and discover new opportunities for yourself and the world around you. Together, we can find the answers for tomorrow. The vacancy is open for internal and external candidates. In case of equal qualifications, internal candidates will be prioritized. At Maastricht University, we prefer to contact potential candidates directly. We therefore kindly ask that no agencies or intermediaries submit offers or approaches. Maastricht University is committed to promoting and nurturing a diverse and inclusive community. We believe that diversity in our staff and student population contributes to the quality of research and education at UM, and strive to enable this through inclusive policies and innovative projects led by teams of staff and students. We encourage you to apply for this position. Job Type: Academic Faculty/Service Center: Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences Closing date: 22-10-2026 FTE min: 0,8 FTE max: 1,0 Salary min: €4925,00 Salary max: €6682,00 ID job: 3805 Job Type: Academic Faculty/Service Center: Faculty of Arts and Social Sciences Closing date: 22-10-2026 FTE min: 0,8 FTE max: 1,0 Salary min: €4925,00 Salary max: €6682,00 ID job: 3805 Apply now » Find similar jobs: Vacatures, All jobs Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab."
    },
